  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nA relatively vigorous shortwave continues to sink southward through \nthe day today doing nothing more than providing a few clouds to the \narea. The column is rather dry as surface high pressure noses in \nfrom the north. Still the area looks entrenched on the western side \nof a rex block as a strong upper low deepens along the east coast \nand a high pressure system sits in southeastern Canada. This block \nhelps to dampen a wave as it approaches from the west Sunday \nnight/Monday and this allows dry weather to continue as another \nsurface high approaches from the west later Monday into Monday \nnight. Finally, a Pacific wave approaches the Northern Plains and \nwestern Great Lakes between Monday and Tuesday helping to kick the \nstalled strong area of low pressure along the east coast out to sea. \n\nThis opens to the door to increased moisture as a mid level wave \nwith its strongest support in the upper jet more in Canada and a \nshearing area of vorticity to its south that tries to edge into the \narea into Tuesday night. The best columnar moisture swings through \nnorthern and western areas late Tuesday night into early Wednesday \nmorning. Models currently only have mid 50s to low 60s dew points \nmoving in during that time, but 850 mb dew points do surpass 10C. \nThe ECMWF has minimal MUCAPE to work with. This all seems to \nindicate issues with moisture working into the region overall and \npotentially some issues with dry air choking moisture transport up \nand to the surface even with a moisture connection to the Gulf.\n\nAfter the prior wave moves through into at least the first part of \nWednesday, it appears there's a bit of a break in rain until the \nwashed out front from Wednesday moves back northward as a warm front \nfor Thursday. This front stalls out across the area and serves as a \nfocus for showers from Thursday into the weekend. Storm potential \nwill be dependent on the eventual location of the front, as \nbetter instability could stay south of the area. Surface dew \npoints reach 65F Thursday and Friday The ECMWF has good large \nscale ascent across the area Thursday AM as well as parts of \nFriday and Saturday. There is still plenty of disagreement \nbetween when a cutoff low in the southwest makes it up here, but\nsometime between Friday and Saturday looks likely. If there was\none day that looks like there could be some stronger \nthunderstorms, it's Saturday, but we have a long time to get \nthere yet.",
